      "hero.eyebrow": "Segmentation & Annotation for Egocentric Human Video with open-source VLM",
      "hero.lede": "",
      "en.banner": "",
      "intro.h2": "Introduction: why egocentric human video needs subtask annotation",
      "intro.ego": "Egocentric human video is typically recorded with a head-mounted camera as a person performs everyday manipulation in a real environment—for example, chopping vegetables in a kitchen, assembling parts at a workbench, or restocking store shelves. The camera follows the wearer's head motion, while the hands and manipulated objects dominate the view. Changes in lighting, occlusions, failed attempts, and retries occur naturally rather than in a staged laboratory setup. Public datasets such as EgoVerse<sup class=\"cite\"><a class=\"cite-ref\" href=\"#ref-egoverse\">8</a></sup>, EgoDex<sup class=\"cite\"><a class=\"cite-ref\" href=\"#ref-egodex\">7</a></sup>, and EgoLive<sup class=\"cite\"><a class=\"cite-ref\" href=\"#ref-egolive\">9</a></sup> have brought the volume of such footage beyond one thousand hours.",
      "intro.value": "The potential value of such footage for robot learning begins with the rich visual, manipulation-semantic, and behavioral priors it can provide<sup class=\"cite\"><a class=\"cite-ref\" href=\"#ref-vitra\">6</a></sup><sup class=\"cite\"><a class=\"cite-ref\" href=\"#ref-egodex\">9</a></sup><sup class=\"cite\"><a class=\"cite-ref\" href=\"#ref-egoverse\">10</a></sup>. Human hands and robot end effectors differ in form, but shared high-level notions such as object state, task intent, and observation-conditioned action still overlap. Human video can therefore support vision-language-action (VLA) representation learning; robot data can then adapt those representations to a particular robot’s observation and action spaces. Compared with teleoperated robot data, real human video also covers a broader range of scenes, objects, and skills, and can be collected in parallel without robot hardware or teleoperators.",
      "intro.task": "Raw egocentric video can train video-generation and world models. When paired with wrist trajectories estimated through hand reconstruction, it can also provide weak action supervision. This supervision is weak because the trajectories describe hand motion only approximately: they contain no robot control commands, contact forces, or actuator states, and they do not map directly to a particular robot embodiment. Nor do these signals explicitly identify which operation is underway or when it is complete. Many robot policies need precisely this time-aligned high-level supervision. A VLA training example typically combines an observation, a language instruction, and an action, with the instruction aligned to the relevant time interval. Hierarchical models such as &pi;<sub>0.5</sub> first predict a high-level subtask instruction and then condition low-level action generation on it<sup class=\"cite\"><a class=\"cite-ref\" href=\"#ref-pi05\">1</a></sup>; such high-level modules can obtain direct supervision from time-aligned subtask labels. Segmentation boundaries also have independent value: they mark the start and end of a completed operation and can directly support success detection, skill retrieval, and dataset curation. Turning long videos into usable supervision therefore requires answering two questions: when each operation starts and ends, and what was completed in that interval. This report calls these subtask segmentation and semantic labeling.",
      "intro.hard": "Automating both outputs is difficult. Egocentric footage moves with the wearer's head, hands often occlude the manipulated object, adjacent actions rarely have clean pauses, and fine-grained operations may unfold continuously. Manual segment-level annotation is expensive, so we need an auditable, reproducible annotation pipeline with controlled cost.",
      "intro.p3": "EgoANT is an automatic annotation pipeline for egocentric human manipulation video: it first divides a long video into action-level segments, then generates a concise operation description for each segment. On HomER's 25 videos and 470 reference segments, the best segmentation configuration reaches a segmentation score (Segment F1) of <strong>0.2031</strong>; semantic labeling on fixed reference boundaries reaches a fixed-boundary labeling accuracy (Label Acc) of <strong>55.7%</strong>; and the best video-only end-to-end configuration reaches an end-to-end score (E2E F1) of <strong>0.1542</strong>. These results demonstrate the feasibility of a reproducible first-person subtask annotation pipeline built entirely on open-weight vision-language models.",
      "tldr.h2": "TL;DR",
      "tldr.flow.cap": "Pipeline overview: segmentation on the left, labeling on the right. Two recommended paths—raw-frame single path, or multi-candidate selection then emit.",
      "tldr.scope": "<strong>Evaluation scope:</strong> HomER, the egocentric-human subset of WGO-Bench, with 25 videos and 470 human-annotated segments. Open-weight Qwen vision-language models generate all boundaries and descriptions; Gemini-3.5-Flash is used only offline as a semantic judge of whether a description matches the human annotation. See <a href=\"#evaluate\">&sect;2</a> for full metric definitions.",
      "tldr.pt1": "<strong>Model ranking is stage-dependent.</strong> On fixed-boundary labeling, Qwen3.6-27B raw frames reach 55.7%; multi-frame collages and heuristic overlays fall to 52.8% and 50.6%. Under matched contact sheets and prompts for segmentation, 27B scores 0.1278 versus 0.0952 for 397B. The best end-to-end score, however, comes from the 397B candidate selector (<strong>0.1542</strong>). So <strong>within the configurations evaluated here</strong>, choosing the model for the stage often matters more than extra visual-input tricks; this does not mean 27B is stronger than 397B across stages.",
      "tldr.pt2": "<strong>The best segmentation recipe is a coarse whole-episode pass followed by local refinement.</strong> The model first scans timestamped grids for the complete video, proposes coarse boundaries, and then re-segments short windows around those boundaries. This raises the segmentation score from 0.0953 for the wrist-speed rule baseline to <strong>0.2031</strong>, yielding 79 valid temporal matches among 470 human reference segments. Macrodata reports a Gemini segmentation score of 0.227 on the same subset, 0.024 above this result.",
      "tldr.pt3": "<strong>Segmentation and labeling need different visual inputs.</strong> Boundary finding benefits from longer temporal context, so timestamped contact sheets help; once boundaries are fixed, the model needs hand and object detail, so raw frames help more. Neighboring segments do not raise fixed-boundary labeling accuracy and instead introduce cross-segment action leakage into the current description.",
      "tldr.pt4": "<strong>Under the current WGO-Bench evaluation protocol, temporal segmentation is the dominant bottleneck.</strong> The best setup predicts 308 segments, but only 79 form valid temporal matches with the 470 human references; 60 of those further pass semantic matching. Most losses happen before description quality is judged, so better action boundaries still offer more headroom than polishing labels.",
      "tldr.pt5": "<strong>End-to-end labeling splits into three tiers: raw baseline, best single-path, and accuracy-max selector.</strong> On the same S2 predicted boundaries, 397B raw frames score <strong>0.1414</strong> (about <strong>$1.1 / video-hour</strong>); the best single-path at similar call cost is 397B ffmpeg frames at <strong>0.1491</strong>; multi-candidate selection reaches <strong>0.1542</strong> (about <strong>$2.7 / video-hour</strong>). Prefer ffmpeg for cost-sensitive runs, raw frames as the simplest baseline, and the selector when maximizing accuracy.",
      "tldr.seg": "Of 470 reference segments, 79 predictions pass temporal matching.",
      "tldr.seg.config": "Setup: Qwen3.6-27B · timestamped grids · coarse pass + local refinement",
      "tldr.label": "Of 470 reference segments with human boundaries fixed, 262 descriptions pass semantic matching.",
